Q: Why does my code for finding the max. depth of a tree fail? A: <p>As explained by @Ikegami, the algorithm is flawed and the result will be smaller than the actual depth in many cases.</p>
<p>Here is classic code to compute the depth using recursion:</p>
<pre><code>static inline int max_int(int a, b) {
    return a &gt;= b ? a : b;
}

int maxDepth(struct TreeNode *root) {
    if (root == NULL) return 0;
    return max_int(1 + maxDepth(root-&gt;left),
                   1 + maxDepth(root-&gt;right));
}
</code></pre>
<p>This algorithm recurses on both arms of each node, which causes a number of function calls greater than the total number of nodes in the tree and a recursion depth just one more than the depth of the tree, which can be very high in case of degenerate trees with very imbalanced distributions.</p>
<p>It is difficult to reduce the number of recursive calls in non trivial cases but using an ancillary function can help avoid very deep recursion in pathological cases:</p>
<pre><code>// return the depth of the subtree at a given level, node is not NULL
static int maxDepth1(int depth, struct TreeNode *node) {
    depth += 1;
    if (node-&gt;right == NULL) {
        if (node-&gt;left == NULL) return depth;
        // tail recursion
        return maxDepth1(depth, node-&gt;left);
    } else
    if (node-&gt;left == NULL) {
        // tail recursion
        return maxDepth1(depth, node-&gt;right);
    } else {
        return max_int(maxDepth1(depth, node-&gt;left),
                       maxDepth1(depth, node-&gt;right));
    }
}

// return the depth of the tree, root can be NULL
int maxDepth(struct TreeNode *root) {
    if (root == NULL) return 0;
    return maxDepth1(0, root);
}
</code></pre>
<p>An optimizing compiler will take advantage of the tail recursions in <code>maxDepth1</code> to convert these calls into a loop:</p>
<pre><code>// return the depth of the subtree at a given level, node is not NULL
static int maxDepth1(int depth, struct TreeNode *node) {
    for (;;) {
        depth += 1;
        if (node-&gt;right == NULL) {
            node = node-&gt;left;
            if (node == NULL) return depth;
        } else
        if (node-&gt;left == NULL) {
            node = node-&gt;right;
        } else {
            return max_int(maxDepth1(depth, node-&gt;left),
                           maxDepth1(depth, node-&gt;right));
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>As commented by @chux, this iterative version no longer needs <code>depth</code> as an argument and the code can be further simplified. Keeping <code>MaxDepth1</code> as an ancillary function reduces the number of tests for <code>NULL</code>.</p>
<pre><code>// return the depth of a subtree, node is never NULL
static int maxDepth1(struct TreeNode *node) {
    for (int depth = 0;;) {
        depth += 1;
        if (node-&gt;right == NULL) {
            node = node-&gt;left;
            if (node == NULL) return depth;
        } else
        if (node-&gt;left == NULL) {
            node = node-&gt;right;
        } else {
            return depth + max_int(maxDepth1(node-&gt;left),
                                   maxDepth1(node-&gt;right));
        }
    }
}

// return the depth of the tree, root can be NULL
int maxDepth(struct TreeNode *root) {
    if (root == NULL) return 0;
    return maxDepth1(root);
}
</code></pre>
<p>This can be simplified again and the number of recursive call reduced using ideas from @chux and @Ikegami. This code also ensures the tree is walked in left recursive order, which is more cache friendly if it was built and allocated in this order:</p>
<pre><code>static inline int max_int(int a, b) {
    return a &gt;= b ? a : b;
}

// return the depth of the tree, root can be NULL
int maxDepth(struct TreeNode *node) {
    int depth1 = 0, depth2 = 0;
    while (node) {
        depth1++;
        if (!node-&gt;right) {
            node = node-&gt;left;
        } else {
            if (node-&gt;left) {
                depth2 = max_int(depth1 + maxDepth(node-&gt;left), depth2);
            }
            node = node-&gt;right;
        }
    }
    return max_int(depth1, depth2);
}
</code></pre>

Q: Ajax based username availability check A: <pre><code>&lt;%@ Page Language=&quot;C#&quot; AutoEventWireup=&quot;true&quot; CodeBehind=&quot;Register.aspx.cs&quot; Inherits=&quot;YourNamespace.Register&quot; %&gt;

&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head runat=&quot;server&quot;&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;Register New User&lt;/title&gt;
    &lt;script src=&quot;https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.6.0.min.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
    &lt;script type=&quot;text/javascript&quot;&gt;
        $(document).ready(function () {
            $('#&lt;%= txtUsername.ClientID %&gt;').blur(function () {
                var username = $(this).val().trim();
                if (username.length &gt; 0) {
                    $.ajax({
                        type: &quot;POST&quot;,
                        url: &quot;Register.aspx/CheckUsername&quot;,
                        data: JSON.stringify({ username: username }),
                        contentType: &quot;application/json; charset=utf-8&quot;,
                        dataType: &quot;json&quot;,
                        success: function (response) {
                            if (response.d) {
                                $('#usernameStatus').text('Username is available').css('color', 'green');
                            } else {
                                $('#usernameStatus').text('Username already exists').css('color', 'red');
                            }
                        },
                        error: function () {
                            $('#usernameStatus').text('Error checking username').css('color', 'orange');
                        }
                    });
                } else {
                    $('#usernameStatus').text('');
                }
            });
        });
    &lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
    &lt;form id=&quot;form1&quot; runat=&quot;server&quot;&gt;
        &lt;div&gt;
            &lt;label&gt;Username:&lt;/label&gt;
            &lt;asp:TextBox ID=&quot;txtUsername&quot; runat=&quot;server&quot;&gt;&lt;/asp:TextBox&gt;
            &lt;span id=&quot;usernameStatus&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
            &lt;label&gt;Password:&lt;/label&gt;
            &lt;asp:TextBox ID=&quot;txtPassword&quot; TextMode=&quot;Password&quot; runat=&quot;server&quot;&gt;&lt;/asp:TextBox&gt;
            &lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
            &lt;asp:Button ID=&quot;btnRegister&quot; Text=&quot;Register&quot; runat=&quot;server&quot; OnClick=&quot;btnRegister_Click&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/form&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>
<pre><code>using System;
using System.Data.SqlClient;
using System.Web.Services;

namespace YourNamespace
{
    public partial class Register : System.Web.UI.Page
    {
        string conString = @&quot;YourConnectionStringHere&quot;;

        protected void Page_Load(object sender, EventArgs e)
        {
        }

        [WebMethod]
        public static bool CheckUsername(string username)
        {
            bool available = false;
            string conString = @&quot;YourConnectionStringHere&quot;;

            using (SqlConnection con = new SqlConnection(conString))
            {
                con.Open();
                string query = &quot;SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Users WHERE Username = @Username&quot;;
                using (SqlCommand cmd = new SqlCommand(query, con))
                {
                    cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(&quot;@Username&quot;, username);
                    int count = (int)cmd.ExecuteScalar();
                    available = count == 0;
                }
            }
            return available;
        }

        protected void btnRegister_Click(object sender, EventArgs e)
        {
            using (SqlConnection con = new SqlConnection(conString))
            {
                con.Open();
                string query = &quot;INSERT INTO Users (Username, Password) VALUES (@Username, @Password)&quot;;
                using (SqlCommand cmd = new SqlCommand(query, con))
                {
                    cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(&quot;@Username&quot;, txtUsername.Text.Trim());
                    cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(&quot;@Password&quot;, txtPassword.Text.Trim());
                    cmd.ExecuteNonQuery();
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
